Text 2: Cognitive psychologist Lena Haraldsen views anthropomorphism primarily as a byproduct of the brain''s pattern-recognition systems rather than a distinct adaptive strategy. According to Haraldsen, the same neural architecture that allows humans to detect faces in clouds and perceive causality in random sequences also generates social attributions toward non-human entities—not because doing so confers survival advantages, but because the system is broadly tuned and occasionally misfires.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Anand treats anthropomorphism as a purposeful evolutionary adaptation, while Haraldsen treats it as an incidental consequence of a general cognitive system"},{"key":"B","text":"Anand believes anthropomorphism occurs only in response to threats, while Haraldsen believes it occurs only in response to social cues"},{"key":"C","text":"Anand argues that anthropomorphism is a modern phenomenon, while Haraldsen argues it has ancient origins"},{"key":"D","text":"Anand focuses on how anthropomorphism affects animals, while Haraldsen focuses on how it affects machines"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Anand frames anthropomorphism as an adaptive cognitive mechanism serving an evolutionary purpose, while Haraldsen frames it as a byproduct of broad pattern-recognition that occasionally misfires. The option capturing this purposeful-adaptation versus incidental-byproduct distinction is correct. The option about threats only versus social cues only is wrong because Anand mentions both threats and allies, and Haraldsen discusses pattern recognition generally rather than social cues exclusively. The option about modern versus ancient is wrong because both authors implicitly discuss the phenomenon''s deep cognitive roots. The option about animals versus machines is wrong because both texts discuss anthropomorphism toward multiple entity types.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);






Text 2: Computational linguist Daisuke Morimoto has developed a rapid-documentation toolkit that combines automated speech recognition, crowd-sourced transcription platforms, and machine-learning-assisted grammatical analysis to produce comprehensive linguistic records in a fraction of the time required by traditional fieldwork. Morimoto acknowledges that his toolkit cannot replace deep ethnographic engagement but argues that for the estimated 3,000 languages facing extinction within the next century, the choice is not between perfect and imperfect documentation but between imperfect documentation and no documentation at all.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"As a well-motivated effort that nevertheless risks producing linguistically incomplete records by prioritizing speed over contextual depth"},{"key":"B","text":"As a fundamentally misguided project that will inevitably cause more harm to endangered languages than it prevents"},{"key":"C","text":"As an approach that succeeds at capturing pragmatic dimensions of language despite its reliance on technology"},{"key":"D","text":"As a complementary methodology that addresses the same aspects of language that immersive fieldwork captures"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Ferreira explicitly warns that efficiency-driven methods risk preserving vocabulary and grammar while losing the communicative ecology of a language. She would likely view Morimoto''s rapid toolkit as well-intentioned but prone to exactly this kind of contextual loss, matching the option about recognizing motivation while noting the risk of incomplete records. The option about causing more harm than it prevents is wrong because Ferreira warns about limitation rather than active damage, and nothing suggests she would deny any value to the documentation produced. The option about succeeding at pragmatic dimensions is wrong because Ferreira''s argument specifically contends that non-immersive methods fail to capture those dimensions. The option about complementary methodology capturing the same aspects is wrong because Ferreira draws a sharp distinction between what immersive and efficiency-driven approaches can capture.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 100, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Subsequent research, however, has complicated this picture. Economist David Neumark reanalyzed the same border region using payroll records rather than telephone surveys and found small but statistically significant employment losses in New Jersey. More recently, a 2019 study of Seattle''s phased minimum-wage increase to fifteen dollars per hour found that while experienced workers saw earnings gains, the total number of low-wage hours worked declined by roughly nine percent, suggesting that employers reduced hours rather than headcount. These findings do not overturn the monopsony explanation entirely, but they indicate that the employment effects of minimum-wage policy depend on the magnitude of the increase, the structure of local labor markets, and the metric used to measure employment.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Although an influential study suggested that minimum-wage increases do not reduce employment, subsequent research indicates that the effects depend on factors such as the size of the increase and how employment is measured."},{"key":"B","text":"Card and Krueger's 1994 study conclusively demonstrated that minimum-wage increases do not cause job losses, a finding that later research has consistently confirmed."},{"key":"C","text":"Economists now agree that the monopsony model fully explains why minimum-wage increases do not reduce employment in the fast-food industry."},{"key":"D","text":"David Neumark's reanalysis of the New Jersey data proved that Card and Krueger's original methodology was fundamentally flawed and their conclusions invalid."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The passage presents Card and Krueger''s 1994 finding that a minimum-wage increase did not reduce employment, then describes subsequent research by Neumark and the Seattle study that found some employment effects under certain conditions. The main idea is that the original finding has been complicated by later evidence showing the effects depend on context. The distractor claiming Card and Krueger''s findings were ''consistently confirmed'' contradicts the passage''s second paragraph, which describes qualifying evidence. The distractor about economists agreeing on the monopsony model overstates the passage, which says the findings ''do not overturn the monopsony explanation entirely'' but introduce qualifications. The distractor about Neumark proving the original study ''fundamentally flawed'' overstates his contribution; the passage says he found ''small but statistically significant employment losses,'' not that the methodology was invalid.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);








- The Virginia opossum (Didelphis virginiana) is the only marsupial native to North America north of Mexico.
- Field studies in rural Georgia recorded opossums consuming over 47 distinct food items in a single season.
- These items included persimmons, earthworms, bird eggs, carrion, garden vegetables, and small rodents.
- Opossums are classified as opportunistic omnivores by mammalian ecologists.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"The Virginia opossum, an opportunistic omnivore, has been documented eating more than 47 distinct food items in a single season, ranging from persimmons and earthworms to bird eggs and small rodents."},{"key":"B","text":"Mammalian ecologists classify the Virginia opossum as an opportunistic omnivore, a designation shared by several other North American mammals."},{"key":"C","text":"The Virginia opossum is notable for being the only marsupial found in North America north of Mexico."},{"key":"D","text":"Field studies conducted in rural Georgia have contributed to scientists' understanding of the Virginia opossum's ecological role."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The goal is to emphasize the breadth of the opossum''s diet. The correct choice achieves this by citing the specific count of 47 distinct food items and listing several diverse examples spanning fruit, invertebrates, eggs, and small mammals. The option focusing on the omnivore classification mentions diet categorically but does not convey the range or variety of foods consumed. The option about the opossum being the only North American marsupial addresses taxonomy, not diet. The option referencing field studies in Georgia is too vague to highlight dietary breadth and instead focuses on research methodology.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 60, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);







The following table summarizes key characteristics of the two groups at baseline:

| Characteristic | Fermented-food group (n=623) | Control group (n=624) |
|---|---|---|
| Mean age (years) | 34.2 | 51.7 |
| Median daily fiber intake (g) | 31.4 | 18.9 |
| Percentage exercising >150 min/week | 72% | 38% |
| Percentage taking probiotic supplements | 8% | 7% |', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"The percentage of participants taking probiotic supplements was nearly identical across both groups."},{"key":"B","text":"The fermented-food group had substantially higher median daily fiber intake and exercise levels than the control group at baseline."},{"key":"C","text":"The mean age of the control group was considerably higher than that of the fermented-food group."},{"key":"D","text":"The sample sizes of the two groups were approximately equal."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'The researchers concluded that fermented food consumption directly enhances gut microbiome diversity, but the study was observational (no random assignment). The finding that the fermented-food group had substantially higher fiber intake and exercise levels at baseline introduces confounding variables—fiber and exercise are independently associated with gut microbiome diversity—thereby undermining the claim of a direct causal link. The finding about similar probiotic supplement usage actually reduces one potential confound, supporting rather than undermining the conclusion. The age difference between groups is a potential confound, but it is a single variable; the combination of higher fiber intake and greater exercise is a more direct challenge because both are independently known to influence gut microbiome diversity. The finding about equal sample sizes is a methodological strength, not a challenge to the conclusion.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"evidence_mismatch"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 110, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);



Narayanan cautions, however, that her findings should not be interpreted as evidence that unionization directly causes higher wages in all sectors. The cities in her sample share particular demographic and economic characteristics—including above-average educational attainment and proximity to state capitals—that may independently contribute to wage growth. Disentangling these factors, she writes, would require longitudinal studies tracking individual workers across union and non-union employment spells.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"It qualifies the first paragraph's findings by identifying confounding variables and methodological constraints"},{"key":"B","text":"It provides additional statistical evidence that strengthens the first paragraph's central claim"},{"key":"C","text":"It shifts focus to a different economist's critique of the methodology described in the first paragraph"},{"key":"D","text":"It applies the first paragraph's findings to a policy recommendation about public-sector wages"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The second paragraph explicitly cautions against over-interpreting the first paragraph''s wage-growth correlation by identifying confounding demographic and economic characteristics and calling for further longitudinal research. This qualifies rather than extends the findings. The option about additional strengthening evidence is wrong because the second paragraph introduces doubt rather than support. The option about a different economist''s critique is wrong because Narayanan herself raises the caveats. The option about a policy recommendation is wrong because the passage proposes further research rather than any specific policy action.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);




Text 2: Economic historian Judith Caplan has shown that significant commercial activity—including fur trading networks, cattle ranching operations, and mining ventures—was already well established in many western territories before any rail lines reached them. Caplan argues that railroads accelerated existing economic patterns rather than generating them from nothing.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"By agreeing that railroads were important but arguing that they built upon pre-existing economic foundations rather than creating markets from scratch"},{"key":"B","text":"By rejecting entirely the idea that railroads had any meaningful effect on the western economy"},{"key":"C","text":"By suggesting that Fell's research focuses on the wrong time period for analyzing western development"},{"key":"D","text":"By proposing that fur trading was more economically significant than railroad construction"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'Text 2 acknowledges that railroads accelerated economic patterns but emphasizes that commercial activity predated the railroads, directly countering Text 1''s claim that railroads created markets where none existed. This aligns with the option about agreeing on importance while disputing the creation-from-scratch framing. The option about rejecting railroads entirely is wrong because Caplan grants they had an accelerating effect. The option about the wrong time period is wrong because Caplan does not challenge Fell''s chronological focus. The option about fur trading being more significant is wrong because Caplan uses fur trading as one example of pre-existing activity, not as a claim of relative economic superiority.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"detail_misread"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 55, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);

- The Pony Express operated from April 1860 to October 1861, covering approximately 1,900 miles between St. Joseph, Missouri, and Sacramento, California.
- The service reduced mail delivery time between the coasts from roughly 24 days by stagecoach to about 10 days by relay riders.
- The Pony Express employed around 183 riders and maintained 190 relay stations during its peak operation.
- The venture''s parent company, Russell, Majors and Waddell, lost an estimated $200,000 on the Pony Express (equivalent to roughly $6.8 million today) and declared bankruptcy in 1861.
- The completion of the transcontinental telegraph in October 1861 rendered the Pony Express obsolete within days.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Despite halving cross-country mail delivery times, the Pony Express proved financially ruinous for its operators: Russell, Majors and Waddell lost approximately $200,000 on the venture and declared bankruptcy the same year the transcontinental telegraph made the service obsolete."},{"key":"B","text":"The Pony Express, which operated from April 1860 to October 1861, employed around 183 riders who carried mail across roughly 1,900 miles of frontier terrain between Missouri and California."},{"key":"C","text":"By reducing coast-to-coast mail delivery from 24 days to approximately 10 days, the Pony Express represented a significant technological achievement in American communications history."},{"key":"D","text":"Russell, Majors and Waddell maintained 190 relay stations for the Pony Express, a logistical undertaking that highlights the enormous operational scale of nineteenth-century mail delivery."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The goal is to challenge the view that the Pony Express was a commercial success. The correct choice does this by conceding the service''s speed improvement (''despite halving delivery times'') while foregrounding the financial losses and bankruptcy that demonstrate commercial failure, and noting the rapid obsolescence caused by the telegraph. The option listing operational details such as the number of riders and the route''s distance provides neutral factual context without addressing commercial outcomes. The option celebrating the reduction in delivery time actually reinforces the conventional view of the Pony Express as an achievement rather than challenging it. The option about the 190 relay stations emphasizes logistical scale, which neither supports nor undermines the claim of commercial success, and omits any financial data.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"inference_overreach"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);





- Lithium-ion batteries currently dominate the grid-scale energy storage market, holding roughly 93 percent of installed capacity worldwide as of 2024.
- Iron-air batteries, a newer technology, use iron oxidation and reduction to store and release energy.
- Lithium-ion batteries typically last 10 to 15 years before significant capacity degradation.
- Iron-air batteries are projected to last 25 to 30 years, but their round-trip energy efficiency is approximately 45 percent, compared to about 85 percent for lithium-ion systems.', $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Although lithium-ion batteries offer higher round-trip efficiency at roughly 85 percent compared to iron-air's 45 percent, iron-air batteries are projected to last up to twice as long, potentially 25 to 30 years versus 10 to 15 years for lithium-ion."},{"key":"B","text":"Iron-air batteries rely on a process of iron oxidation and reduction that distinguishes them from the lithium-ion chemistry used in batteries that currently dominate the market."},{"key":"C","text":"Lithium-ion batteries account for approximately 93 percent of installed grid-scale energy storage capacity, making them the most widely deployed battery technology worldwide."},{"key":"D","text":"Newer battery technologies, including iron-air systems, may eventually challenge the dominance of lithium-ion batteries in the energy storage market."}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'A', NULL, E'The goal is to present a contrast between the two battery technologies. The correct choice accomplishes this by directly comparing two specific performance dimensions — round-trip efficiency and lifespan — using concrete figures for each technology and a contrastive structure (''Although ... compared to''). The option about iron oxidation and reduction describes how iron-air batteries work but does not compare the two technologies on any shared metric. The option about lithium-ion''s 93 percent market share provides information about only one technology and does not contrast it with the other. The option about newer technologies challenging lithium-ion dominance makes a speculative claim without presenting the specific contrasting data points from the notes.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"rhetorical_purpose_error"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 75, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);
